
<html>
<head><title> Kravchuk:Introduction Krawtchouk_Polynomials </title></head>
<body>
<p>
<H2> Krawtchouk_Polynomials </H2>
<p>
 
Krawtchouk <i> polynomial</i> is a mere different view at the Krawtchouk <i> matrix</i>.
Pick up a row in \FFF n{}, and consider its entries as a
function of the column's index as the variable.
This function will be called a <i> Krawtchouk polynomial</i>.
Thus every matrix K<sup> n </sup> of order n defines n+1 Krawtchouk polynomials
indexed by row index p, and denoted by K<sup> (n) </sup>_p(j).
<p>
For example, Krawtchouk matrix of order n=3 defines four Krawtchouk
polynomials indexed by p=0,1,2,3 and denoted by K<sup> (n) </sup>_p(j), where
j is an integer (or real) variable.  For instance, values of K_2(j) are
<p>
3 \quad 1 \quad -1 \quad -3
<p>
for j=0,1,2,3 respectively.
<p>
<!--  \caption{  Krawtchouk polynomials as rows in Krawtchouk matrices} --><p>
 
<dl><dt> <b> Definition </b> <dd> 
A p-th Krawtchouk polynomial of order N is <br>
       <sup> N </sup> K<sup> _ </sup>p(j) =: <sup> N </sup>K<sub> pj </sub>
</dl>
<p>
<b> Problem.</b>  Show that this function is indeed a polynomial in j.
Note also that the degree of <sup> N </sup>K_p(j) is p.
 
\begin{ex}{\rm Check that the following polynomials indeed reconstructthe rows of matrix K<sup> 3 </sup>.}
\end{ex}
<p>
 
<p>
In order to ``center'' the polynomials around the mid-value of the index
j, we may consider a change of variable into x=i-j.  This
composition will be also called Krawtchouk polynomial and will be denoted
<p>
k<sup> (n) </sup>_p (x) = K<sup> (n) </sup>_p(i-x) = K<sup> (n) </sup>_p({n-x\over 2})
<p>
Clearly, k_p(x) is also a polynomial in x of degree p.
<p>
 
<b> Problem.</b> Show that the two forms of Krawtchouk polynomials are:
<p>
<table align=center><tr><td align=center>K<sup> (n) </sup>(j) <td align=right>=<td align=right>\sum<sub> a=0 </sub><sup> p </sup> (-)<sup> p </sup> {i\choose {p-a}} {j\choose a}</tr>
<tr><td align=right>  k<sup> (n) </sup>(x) <td align=right>=<td align=right>...</tr>
</table>
<p>
 Graphs of Krawtchouk polynomials.
<p>
This is a matter of convention which is chosen as the definition of Krawtchouk
polynomials; we shall continue rather with symmetrized version K.  The
crucial property of Krawtchouk polynomials is that the family of n+1 polynomialsof order n forms an orthogonal basis in n dimensional space they
span, where the scalar product has binary coefficients on diagonal,
i.e.:
<p>
\dirac{K_p}{K_q}  = \sum_x {n \choose x} K_p(x) K_q(x)
                   = \delta<sub> p\;q </sub>
<p>
for any two polynomials of order n. This orthogonal system ....
<p>
 
\begin{rem}{\rm  Polynomials in v of the algebraic mnemotechnic rule (1)form {columns} of K while Krawtchouk polynomials (in x or j) form
its <i> rows</i>, and these two should not be confused.}
\end{rem}
<p>

